body:
While not required by the assignment, maybe these duplicated output statements would be better written as an overloaded `<<` operator on an `ostream`? That way, you could print out the same content with only two statements:
```
cout << p1 << endl;
cout << p2 << endl;
```

initializer lists for constructor would be and efficient way of doing things, ie

Package(ID_type id, Weight_type w, string d) 
    : trackingID(id), weight(w), dest(d) 
{
    // empty body
}

I think it is up to preference but it is another way to "level up" in a way.

Everything look good, I can only think that maybe make the constructor into two lines for readability. 
Package(IDType id, WeightType w, string d)
    : trackingID(id), weight(w), dest(d) {}

SmartWatch(BatteryLifeType b, VersionType v, string m)
    : batteryLife(b), version(v), model(m)
{
}
would be another way of doing this constructor

Rather than using string concatenation, you should be able to instead chain stream insertion operators. For example:
```cpp
cout << "A" + "B" + "C" << endl;
cout << "A" << "B" << "C" << endl;
```
Perhaps this may be more efficient? Since using the + operator may cause reallocations, putting the data into the output stream directly could be less work.
